wrong[['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']]: That's what recursive DFS gives. Here A marks C as soon as it pushes it, so when B is explored, C counts as seen and only D is pushed. D, on top, comes out before C.
wrong[['A', 'C', 'B', 'D']]: A pushes C first and B last, in reverse, so B is on top and is explored first.
wrong[['A', 'B', 'D', 'C', 'C']]: Marking on push means each vertex is pushed at most once: C is never pushed a second time.
hint: When A is explored, which vertices does it mark?
hint: When B is explored, which of its neighbours are still unmarked?
right: `['A', 'B', 'D', 'C']`. Marking when pushed lets A claim C before B can reach it, so B can't dive on to C; it goes to D instead. True depth-first order is A, B, C, D, so this isn't a depth-first search any more.
wrong: A pushes and marks C and B. B is popped; only D is unmarked, so D is pushed and popped next. Finally C, which A pushed long ago, comes off.

=== task: cells medium
title: The top of the stack
prompt: This is the reversed-push stack DFS from the lesson, on the lesson's graph, starting at A. Fill in each box, looking at the stack right after the named vertex's neighbours have been pushed.
code:
```python
graph = {"A": ["B", "C"], "B": ["D", "A", "E"], "C": ["A", "F"],
         "D": ["B"], "E": ["B", "F"], "F": ["C", "E"]}

seen = set()
stack = ["A"]
while stack:
    v = stack.pop()
    if v in seen:
